# The script `mise run ffi-test` has the headless mode of an editor built
# from the core alone run (the set `tests/empty-set`, which is the default
# set with its packages, the shipped themes among them, dropped), against the
# config in `config/`, which defines one theme, `first`, and names none.
# What it prints must be exactly `expected`. It touches no file.

reload
settle

# A key makes the frame. No config names a theme, so the scene is drawn in
# `default`, the core's built-in theme: the scene line ends with the
# theme's name.
key <Escape> Escape -
scene

# `load-theme`, called with no name, asks for one, with the registered
# themes as its candidates. With nothing typed the candidate list, the
# view after the minibuffer's, holds every theme: `default`, which the core
# registers, and config's `first`, and no shipped palette. The completion
# styles and displays are a default package too (packages/completion/), so
# the list is drawn by the core's own display (`Completion.Display.unregistered`):
# each row its text after two spaces, nothing chosen, with no faces.
run load-theme
scene

# `first` is typed and `<Enter>` pressed. The minibuffer's commands are a
# default package too (packages/minibuffer/), so `<Enter>` runs
# `minibuffer-accept`, which nothing in this editor registers: the echo
# area says there is no such command, the prompt stays open and focused
# with `first` typed and listed, and the theme in force stays `default`.
# The default set's editor accepts, and draws the next scene in `first`.
commit first
key <Enter> Enter -
scene

# `load-theme` is run again and a shipped palette's name typed: the
# prompt that is open takes the keys, so its minibuffer holds
# `firstsolarized-dark`, which no theme matches, and `<Enter>` again finds
# no `minibuffer-accept`. The default set's editor loads the palette.
run load-theme
commit solarized-dark
key <Enter> Enter -
scene
